<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">All Saints' Church, Brixworth</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">7th–8th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">All Saints' Church in Brixworth, Northamptonshire, is one of the largest and best-preserved <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> churches in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. Built probably in the late 7th or 8th century, partly with reused Roman bricks and tiles, it was a basilica with side chambers and later gained a ring crypt and tower. It is a key monument of the Mercian church.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/all-saints-church-brixworth.jpg" alt="All Saints' Church, Brixworth" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Simon Burchell · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:All_Saints%27_Church,_Brixworth_89.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
